-# Decisions for 66387591ebe6
-
-All decisions below concern the selected generator only. The canonical Racket authority and canonical examples were left unchanged. “Original rationale unknown” means the v1.3.0 source contained the behavior but no task-linked justification for it.
-
-## D1 — Preserve the authority; replace the curriculum
-
-**Choice.** Keep canonical rule hash `60007e76f218` and repair only generator-side data, validation, tests and documentation.
-
-**Why.** The four canonical pairs, selected Racket execution, and authority-sync check already agreed. The defects were in the sampler and its contract: scattered cells, unrelated dimensions, ignored controls, and absent semantic witnesses.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** I did not alter the Racket rule to accommodate baseline outputs, and I did not edit canonical examples. Either would turn a generator defect into a reference change without evidence.
-
-**Probe.** Final `rule_smoke` is 120/120 Racket applications and 120/120 Python-mirror parity; final `authority_sync` is green. Evidence: `checks/after-final/checks/66387591ebe6_after_rule_smoke.json` and `..._authority_sync.json`.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** The archival bank still has an already-reported divergent copy; selected execution parity is green. The repair makes no claim about that archival artifact.
-
-## D2 — Use 9 and 11 as exact dimensions
-
-**Choice.** `grid_h` and `grid_w` each accept exactly 9 or 11. Ordinary slots remain square to track the canonical examples; rectangular 9×11 and 11×9 scenes are available through exact overrides.
-
-**Original behavior/rationale.** The baseline sampled broader dimensions and sometimes failed to honor requests; its task-specific rationale was unknown.
-
-**Why.** All four canonical inputs are 9×9 or 11×11. These sizes leave room for every radius-three rotation from every declared pivot region while retaining exact canonical profiles.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Keeping 7–14 broadened canvas size without adding semantic value and contributed to zero canonical-profile support. Scheduling all four rectangular combinations would improve nuisance diversity but depart more often from the public profile; I retained them as tested overrides instead.
-
-**Probe.** The high-volume census contains 750 ordinary 9×9 and 750 ordinary 11×11 pairs; override tests cover independent height and width choices.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** Ordinary data correlates height and width. A downstream curriculum that wants rectangles must request them explicitly.
-
-## D3 — One connected 3- or 4-cell object
-
-**Choice.** Generate one 4-connected object of three or four cells, spanning at least two rows and two columns, with every offset within Chebyshev radius three of the pivot.
-
-**Original behavior/rationale.** The baseline placed unrelated singleton cells over the canvas. No rationale linked that distribution to the canonical examples.
-
-**Why.** Canonical inputs use a small connected shape near the pivot. Connectivity makes the transformed entity visually coherent while retaining enough asymmetry to expose direction and center.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Scattered points were off-profile; a single fixed polyomino caused excessive repetition; arbitrary larger objects would expand beyond the observed grammar. The selected family starts from canonical and nearby asymmetric shapes, applies D4 transforms, then translates them lawfully.
-
-**Probe.** The first repaired vocabulary failed a 90% uniqueness requirement at 1,199/1,500 inputs (`checks/lesson-probe-100-per-band.json`). Expanded lawful presentations reached 1,465/1,500 (`...-v2.json`) while all 1,500 remained strict.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** The curriculum does not teach arbitrary object counts, disconnected objects, or objects farther than radius three.
-
-## D4 — Difficulty changes radius, not the rule
-
-**Choice.** Easy draws choose shapes within radius two; medium and hard allow radius three. All bands share the same authority and exact axes.
-
-**Why.** Radius gives a low-cost visual-complexity distinction without changing the logical operation or creating band-specific answers.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Changing object count, key meaning, or palette semantics by band would create a nuisance shortcut. Making medium and hard use separate artificial rules was rejected.
-
-**Probe.** The 100-lessons-per-band census has 100/100 identifiable lessons in each band and 494, 497, and 497 unique inputs respectively.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** Medium and hard differ only through access to radius-three placements, so they are not strongly separated difficulty tiers.
-
-## D5 — Five pixel-observable pivot regions
-
-**Choice.** The pivot can be at center or one cell toward northwest, northeast, southwest or southeast.
-
-**Why.** Canonical examples center the pivot, but center-only data makes “rotate around grid center” indistinguishable from the true rule. Small one-cell displacements preserve canonical scale and keep every declared shape/rotation in bounds.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Arbitrary pivots near edges need more rejection logic and can confound rotation with clipping. Center-only was retained only as one of five values.
-
-**Probe.** Each region appears 300 times in the 1,500-pair census. The grid-center shortcut fits all four canonical pairs but disagrees on 1,200 generated pairs and survives zero complete lessons.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** The generator does not teach pivots near the canvas boundary.
-
-## D6 — Monochrome canonical branch plus scheduled multicolor witness
-
-**Choice.** `texture=mono` uses one object color; `texture=multicolor` alternates two object colors. The ordinary four-slot schedule is mono, mono, multicolor, mono.
-
-**Why.** Monochrome reproduces the canonical palette structure. One multicolor demo per complete lesson is the smallest addition that proves colors are transported cell-by-cell rather than flattened after rotation.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** All-monochrome leaves a canonical-fitting shortcut. Fully random colors on every cell would move farther from the examples and make shape tracking noisier. Decorative background texture was rejected because the authority rebuilds a zero canvas.
-
-**Probe.** Multicolor appears in 375/1,500 census pairs. The flatten-color rival fits all four canonical pairs, disagrees on exactly those 375 pairs, and survives zero complete lessons.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** Multicolor is correlated with one schedule slot and ordinary key 4; a richer future curriculum could decorrelate nuisance axes while keeping per-lesson witness guarantees.
-
-## D7 — Exclude key 1 from ordinary materialized lessons, retain exact support
-
-**Choice.** Ordinary keys follow `(2,3,4,2)`. `key=1` remains a valid exact override and returns the input unchanged as required by zero quarter-turns.
-
-**Why.** A four-demo ordinary lesson needs the three nonidentity turns, and the generic `rule_smoke` stage rejects identity outputs when canonical training contains no identity pair. The authority nonetheless defines key 1, so removing it from the axis would be dishonest.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Pretending key 1 rotates once changes the rule. Advertising only keys 2–4 erases lawful authority support. Including key 1 ordinarily causes the shared gate failure retained in `checks/after/` and `checks/tdd-red-identity2-command.log`.
-
-**Probe.** Thirty-six exact key-1 draws across all bands produced 36 identity outputs, observed key 1 in all 36, and disagreed with the “keys 2–4 only” extrapolation rival in all 36.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** Ordinary training alone cannot teach key 1. This is explicitly documented and is the basis of shared-tool feedback F2.
-
-## D8 — Rebuild rather than overlay
-
-**Choice.** The mirror and lesson competitors treat the output as a fresh zero canvas containing only key, pivot and rotated object.
-
-**Why.** This is the selected Racket behavior. Overlaying leaves source cells and can appear plausible on symmetric or colliding scenes.
-
-**Alternative rejected.** In-place movement/overlay was implemented only as an executable rival.
-
-**Probe.** The overlay rival disagrees on all 1,500 census pairs and survives zero lessons.
-
-## D9 — Exact axes and unknown-key rejection
-
-**Choice.** Advertise only six axes that can be recovered from pixels: `grid_h`, `grid_w`, `key`, `n_cells`, `pivot_region`, and `texture`. Every Cartesian combination is declared compatible. Reject unknown axes and invalid difficulty values.
-
-**Original behavior/rationale.** The baseline advertised controls that were ignored, aliased or could construct invalid scenes; unknown controls were accepted.
-
-**Why.** Training metadata must describe visible data, not RNG intent. `observe_overrides` therefore analyzes returned pixels.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Hidden metadata observers, best-effort controls, and large incompatible-combination tables were rejected. The construction supports every declared combination directly.
-
-**Probe.** Final override gate: 12,240/12,240 accepted attempts over 136 cases and three bands, zero findings, unknown-key verdict `rejected`.
-
-## D10 — Four demonstrations and executable rivals
-
-**Choice.** Require four demos and declare eight competitors: identity, fixed clockwise, key-direct turns, counterclockwise, grid-center rotation, overlay, erase-pivot, and color flattening. Declare the last two canonical-fitting shortcuts actually discovered from the public pairs.
-
-**Why.** Individual witness frequency does not prove a complete lesson identifies the rule. Four consecutive slots are the minimum schedule that covers keys 2/3/4 and a multicolor witness while also including an off-center pivot.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Independent random examples can form unlucky windows. A prose-only semantic description is not testable. Hard-coding rival names into the external probe risks drift.
-
-**Probe.** `lesson_identifiable` executes rivals against complete demos. The 300-lesson census tests all cyclic start residues modulo four; every rival has positive pair disagreements and zero surviving lessons.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** This is a finite rival library, not a proof against every possible shortcut. Shared feedback F1 proposes automated rival synthesis to broaden it.
-
-## D11 — Independent mirror for volume, Racket for authority
-
-**Choice.** Use `rule_mirror` for the 1,500-pair census and Racket-backed stages for parity and authority checks.
-
-**Why.** Calling Racket for every high-volume rival comparison is needlessly expensive. The mirror makes exhaustive lesson assembly practical.
-
-**Alternative rejected.** Mirror-only evidence could silently validate the same implementation error. Racket-only high-volume probing would be slower and harder for future agents to iterate.
-
-**Probe.** Final rule smoke reports 120/120 mirror parity; semantic gate and authority sync are also Racket-backed. The lesson probe records this oracle split explicitly.
-
-**Failure mode/limit.** Parity was sampled, not mathematically proved over every legal grid.
-
-## D12 — Determinism and presentation diversity
-
-**Choice.** Use `gen_ctx` with named RNG streams and select from precomputed lawful shape presentations. Same seed/index/difficulty/overrides yields the same pixels.
-
-**Why.** Determinism makes failures reproducible; named streams reduce accidental coupling when axes are overridden. Large lawful presentation pools improve data value without adding new semantics.
-
-**Alternatives rejected.** Global RNG state, unbounded rejection sampling, and lowering the uniqueness threshold after the first failed census were rejected.
-
-**Probe.** Task-local tests verify determinism. The retained red diversity run proves the test could fail; the final result is 97.67% unique inputs.
